module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Birational.Birational
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.FiniteType
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing

@[expose] public section

universe u
open CategoryTheory Topology
namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Integral affine schemes of finite type over an affine base are birational over that
base when their fraction rings are isomorphic compatibly with the base sections.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0552 (Lemma 29.51.7).
Proof: Affineness identifies the schemes with spectra of their section rings B and C.
These are domains, and are finitely generated algebras over the base section ring A.
Use the given isomorphism to embed both B and C in one fraction field L, fixing A.
Let D be the A-subalgebra of L generated by B and C. Write the finitely many chosen
C-generators as fractions in B, and multiply their nonzero denominators to obtain
b in B. Then B[1/b] = D[1/b]. Similarly choose nonzero c in C with C[1/c] = D[1/c].
Inside D[1/b], express c as d/b^m, with d in B nonzero; inside D[1/c], express b as
a/c^n, with a in C nonzero. Consequently D[1/b,1/c] equals both B[1/(bd)] and
C[1/(ca)]. Taking spectra gives isomorphic principal opens over A. Nonzero elements
of domains define dense opens, so this is a partial isomorphism over the base.

(blueprint: lem-sbc-affine-birational-fraction-ring-equiv) -/
theorem birationalOver_of_fractionRing_equiv
    {X Y S : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine X] [IsAffine Y] [IsAffine S]
    [IsIntegral X] [IsIntegral Y]
    (sX : X ⟶ S) (sY : Y ⟶ S)
    [LocallyOfFiniteType sX] [LocallyOfFiniteType sY]
    (e : FractionRing Γ(X, ⊤) ≃+* FractionRing Γ(Y, ⊤))
    (he : ∀ a : Γ(S, ⊤),
      e (algebraMap Γ(X, ⊤) (FractionRing Γ(X, ⊤)) (sX.appTop.hom a)) =
        algebraMap Γ(Y, ⊤) (FractionRing Γ(Y, ⊤)) (sY.appTop.hom a)) :
    Scheme.BirationalOver sX sY := by sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
